The strategy for this policy is to play “shortest‑path Hex” with tactical defense: it models the board as a weighted graph and uses Dijkstra to estimate how many empty cells are still needed to connect our two sides (cost 0 through our stones, cost 1 through empties, and blocked through opponent stones). Each turn it:
1) finds a current shortest path for both players and uses the empty cells on those paths (plus nearby neighbor cells and simple “bridge” candidates) as a focused candidate set;
2) if the opponent has an immediate winning threat, it identifies the actual winning cells and blocks one;
3) otherwise, it chooses the move that best improves our shortest path while worsening the opponent’s, with small tie‑breaks (center/critical path cells).
